{
 "version": 1,
 "notes": "The village's storylines (GDD D-56): fixed goals, many ways, real conflicts. Each story has people (owner, helps, opposes), stages (what the stranger comes to know or see happen: the Stories tab shows only these), and outcomes that exclude each other (the first reached closes the story) with effects on people's regard and on how they feel about each other. There are no methods anywhere: every condition is a result in the world, checked by code. moves are things a person may choose to do in conversation (the voice picks them, like any move) once code says they're possible; would_agree_if is their own price for it (their motive, like a guard's reasons to let someone through), which they weigh, and name when it isn't met. Conditions are the rule language (backend/rules.py, D-58): {\"kind\": ...} with all / any / not; learned (any of: fact:id or secret:id the stranger has been told); installed (thing); told (the stranger told npc one of these secrets); move (id: a story move was made); seen_demonstration (a working demonstration seen by one of npcs, optionally of something with these flags, optionally this wonder); given (the stranger gave one of npc something of this material or name); treated (npc's condition was treated); regard (npc's axis at_least); knows_story (story has reached stage). Effects (backend/scenario.py): shift_regard (npc: trust, suspicion, respect, awe), shift_opinion (npc of another: warmth).",
